1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,899
Part 2, job by job.

2
00:00:02,219 --> 00:00:05,675
First, build container:
the lecture builds it first,

3
00:00:05,675 --> 00:00:10,859
so every check runs in the same environment,
and build problems show up early.

4
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,724
The lecture speaks of one remote CI server.

5
00:00:14,044 --> 00:00:17,026
GitHub gives you a new one for every job:

6
00:00:17,026 --> 00:00:20,444
runs-on:
ubuntu-latest, six times in this file.

7
00:00:20,764 --> 00:00:22,983
Each job starts on an empty machine.

8
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:26,228
The README's build job
builds the image once,

9
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:27,939
tagged with the commit ID.

10
00:00:28,259 --> 00:00:32,150
It saves it as a tar file
and uploads it as an artifact,

11
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:35,277
so every other job gets
the exact same image.

12
00:00:35,517 --> 00:00:38,952
On GitHub, click a job,
then a step, to open its log.

13
00:00:39,272 --> 00:00:45,928
Docker builds the image from the Dockerfile,
and names it cheese-app-api:<commit ID>.

14
00:00:46,248 --> 00:00:49,170
That tag means this commit's image,
nothing else.

15
00:00:49,417 --> 00:00:52,797
A small slip:
the README promises Buildx caching,

16
00:00:52,797 --> 00:00:57,556
but the build runs on the default builder,
and nothing saves a cache,

17
00:00:57,556 --> 00:01:00,660
so every fresh machine
rebuilds from scratch.

18
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:02,622
The exercises show how.

19
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:08,371
docker save folds the image into one file,
about 176 megabytes.

20
00:01:08,691 --> 00:01:12,638
The upload step carries it
to the artifact store, kept for one day.

21
00:01:12,958 --> 00:01:14,877
Then the build machine is thrown away.

22
00:01:15,117 --> 00:01:17,122
Now step into a fresh check job.

23
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:20,920
An extra command,
docker images, shows just the header.

24
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:22,498
No image at all.

25
00:01:22,733 --> 00:01:28,454
So every check job downloads the artifact
and runs docker load -i on the tar:

26
00:01:28,454 --> 00:01:31,501
the same tag, unpacked from the suitcase.

27
00:01:31,821 --> 00:01:33,357
That's artifact sharing.

28
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:36,078
And the artifact expires after a day,

29
00:01:36,078 --> 00:01:39,843
so re-running a failed job
tomorrow fails at the download.

30
00:01:40,083 --> 00:01:41,448
Your turn to predict.

31
00:01:41,768 --> 00:01:45,480
Delete the "Load Docker image"
step from the unit tests job.

32
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,041
Which step fails, and with what error?

33
00:01:56,883 --> 00:01:58,419
The "Run unit tests" step.

34
00:01:58,739 --> 00:02:02,708
Docker looks for the image
on the machine and finds nothing,

35
00:02:02,708 --> 00:02:05,997
then asks Docker Hub,
where no such image exists:

36
00:02:05,997 --> 00:02:07,272
pull access denied.

37
00:02:07,592 --> 00:02:12,201
Exit code 125:
the step fails, so the job fails.

38
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:15,307
Next, lint and format.

39
00:02:15,627 --> 00:02:18,536
The lecture's coding standards
are Black here,

40
00:02:18,536 --> 00:02:21,003
and its static code analysis is Flake8.

41
00:02:21,323 --> 00:02:24,989
Remember lecture 9:
a formatter rewrites your code;

42
00:02:24,989 --> 00:02:26,571
a linter only reports.

43
00:02:26,817 --> 00:02:32,129
Both run inside the image,
on the api/ folder, at 120 columns.

44
00:02:32,449 --> 00:02:35,990
The Black step says continue-on-error,
false: it blocks.

45
00:02:36,310 --> 00:02:41,707
The Flake8 step says true: it's advisory,
a warning that never fails the job.

46
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:43,337
How does a step fail?

47
00:02:43,657 --> 00:02:46,003
Every command ends with an exit code.

48
00:02:46,323 --> 00:02:47,817
Zero opens the gate.

49
00:02:48,137 --> 00:02:49,428
Anything else shuts it,

50
00:02:49,428 --> 00:02:52,851
and the red climbs from the step
to the job to the whole run.

51
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:56,791
Run 1, the Black step:
would reformat utils.py.

52
00:02:57,111 --> 00:02:58,988
1 file would be reformatted.

53
00:02:59,308 --> 00:03:01,548
Exit code 1, so the gate shuts.

54
00:03:01,783 --> 00:03:03,938
The step list shows what happened next.

55
00:03:04,258 --> 00:03:05,388
Black, failure.

56
00:03:05,708 --> 00:03:07,010
Flake8, skipped.

57
00:03:07,330 --> 00:03:11,831
The steps below a failed step don't run,
unless they ask to run always.

58
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:17,532
The cause is the mess you met in lecture 9,
still in the repository on purpose:

59
00:03:17,532 --> 00:03:22,029
no space after the comma,
and three blank lines where two belong.

60
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:24,251
What would the skipped Flake8 have said?

61
00:03:24,571 --> 00:03:27,856
An extra run shows two warnings
on those same lines.

62
00:03:28,176 --> 00:03:32,016
But even then it's advisory:
only Black decides the job.

63
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:34,624
So that's the whole rule: a step fails

64
00:03:34,624 --> 00:03:37,498
when its command exits
with anything but zero.

65
00:03:37,818 --> 00:03:39,973
It's the only thing CI listens to.

66
00:03:41,517 --> 00:03:42,669
Now the tests.

67
00:03:42,989 --> 00:03:46,120
The lecture's one test box
is three jobs here,

68
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:49,048
side by side, each at a different altitude.

69
00:03:49,284 --> 00:03:53,935
Unit tests check one function at a time,
with no outside dependencies.

70
00:03:54,255 --> 00:03:57,369
The log collects 11 tests, and 11 pass.

71
00:03:57,617 --> 00:04:01,500
Then the coverage step runs them again,
and all 11 pass again.

72
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:04,046
But pytest hits an internal error:

73
00:04:04,046 --> 00:04:07,452
permission denied,
writing into the coverage folder.

74
00:04:07,772 --> 00:04:08,966
Exit code 3.

75
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:10,672
Yet the job is green.

76
00:04:10,992 --> 00:04:14,853
The step says continue-on-error,
so the failure is shunted aside.

77
00:04:15,173 --> 00:04:18,578
Then the upload, marked if:
always(), finds no files;

78
00:04:18,578 --> 00:04:20,891
on GitHub, a yellow warning says so.

79
00:04:21,134 --> 00:04:26,873
The README has a small gap:
it promises terminal, XML and HTML reports.

80
00:04:27,193 --> 00:04:30,329
Docker creates
the missing coverage folder as root,

81
00:04:30,329 --> 00:04:33,465
and the container's user,
app, can't write into it.

82
00:04:33,785 --> 00:04:37,902
Create the folder writable first;
the exercises have the fix.

83
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:41,651
Integration tests use
the FastAPI test client:

84
00:04:41,651 --> 00:04:45,382
the app is called inside
the same Python process.

85
00:04:45,702 --> 00:04:48,625
No server, no port. 10 passed.

86
00:04:48,867 --> 00:04:51,043
System tests need the real thing.

87
00:04:51,363 --> 00:04:53,362
One container starts the server

88
00:04:53,362 --> 00:04:56,974
in the background and publishes
port 9000 on the runner.

89
00:04:57,294 --> 00:04:58,612
An extra command,

90
00:04:58,612 --> 00:05:02,179
docker ps, shows it running,
with 9000 mapped.

91
00:05:02,417 --> 00:05:08,241
Then the job waits 10 seconds,
and curl -f knocks on localhost:9000.

92
00:05:08,561 --> 00:05:12,508
Only when the welcome message
comes back does the next gate open.

93
00:05:12,750 --> 00:05:16,496
A second container runs the tests
with --network host:

94
00:05:16,496 --> 00:05:21,283
it shares the runner's network,
so localhost:9000 reaches the server.

95
00:05:21,603 --> 00:05:22,692
5 passed.

96
00:05:22,934 --> 00:05:28,432
Two steps marked if: always() come last,
even after a failure: show the server log,

97
00:05:28,432 --> 00:05:33,003
where every test request arrives,
then stop and remove the container.

98
00:05:33,323 --> 00:05:35,904
That's the README's conditional execution.

99
00:05:36,150 --> 00:05:40,075
In lecture 9, a missing server
made these tests skip quietly.

100
00:05:40,395 --> 00:05:43,276
Here the curl gate
makes it fail loudly instead.

101
00:05:44,917 --> 00:05:46,397
Last job: the summary,

102
00:05:46,397 --> 00:05:51,040
which the lecture says collects
the results and reports pass or fail.

103
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:53,834
Its needs line names the three test jobs.

104
00:05:54,154 --> 00:05:57,729
And if: always() lets it run
even when one of them failed:

105
00:05:57,729 --> 00:06:00,256
it runs, which isn't the same as passing.

106
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:02,931
Its script prints each job's result,

107
00:06:02,931 --> 00:06:06,307
writes a status
to $GITHUB_OUTPUT for later steps,

108
00:06:06,307 --> 00:06:09,311
and exits with 1
if any result isn't success.

109
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:14,393
In run 1, three success tokens arrive:
unit, integration, system.

110
00:06:14,713 --> 00:06:16,014
All tests passed.

111
00:06:16,250 --> 00:06:18,085
And here is the hook, answered.

112
00:06:18,405 --> 00:06:22,159
Lint isn't in needs,
so its failure never reaches the summary.

113
00:06:22,479 --> 00:06:23,757
The run is still red,

114
00:06:23,757 --> 00:06:26,618
because any failed job
turns the whole run red.

115
00:06:26,867 --> 00:06:30,951
The README says the summary checks
the results of all test jobs,

116
00:06:30,951 --> 00:06:34,078
and that's literally true:
lint isn't a test job.

117
00:06:34,398 --> 00:06:38,408
Add lint and format to needs
and to the check if you want it counted.

118
00:06:38,650 --> 00:06:40,143
Now the bug from class.

119
00:06:40,463 --> 00:06:41,661
In the demo commit,

120
00:06:41,661 --> 00:06:46,138
the distance function takes
the square root of 2 times z, instead of z.

121
00:06:46,458 --> 00:06:48,677
We ran this one for you: just watch.

122
00:06:48,917 --> 00:06:50,346
Predict before the run.

123
00:06:50,666 --> 00:06:54,016
Which jobs turn red now,
and what does Test Summary say?

124
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:05,943
Integration: red, 1 failed and 9 passed.

125
00:07:06,263 --> 00:07:10,232
The distance for 3 and 4 came back
as about 7, not 5.

126
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:13,933
System: red, 2 failed and 3 passed:
3 and 4 again,

127
00:07:13,933 --> 00:07:18,510
and the default point, 1 and 2,
which came back too large as well.

128
00:07:18,830 --> 00:07:22,904
Unit: green, because only
the power function has unit tests.

129
00:07:23,224 --> 00:07:25,998
Lint: red anyway, for utils.py.

130
00:07:26,234 --> 00:07:29,861
And the summary now says some tests failed,
and exits with 1.

131
00:07:30,181 --> 00:07:31,887
This time the summary heard it.

132
00:07:33,433 --> 00:07:35,694
Back to run 1 and the lecture's loop.

133
00:07:36,014 --> 00:07:37,987
The notification came back red,

134
00:07:37,987 --> 00:07:41,425
so now the developer fixes
the code and commits again,

135
00:07:41,425 --> 00:07:43,652
and the loop goes around once more.

136
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,293
The fix is the tool from lecture 9,

137
00:07:46,293 --> 00:07:50,257
Part 4 of the same README,
so these steps are extras here.

138
00:07:50,577 --> 00:07:53,759
pip install pre-commit,
then pre-commit install,

139
00:07:53,759 --> 00:07:56,145
which puts the hook into .git/hooks.

140
00:07:56,383 --> 00:07:58,538
Then pre-commit run --all-files.

141
00:07:58,858 --> 00:08:00,656
The hygiene checks pass;

142
00:08:00,656 --> 00:08:05,300
Black fails, because it modified a file:
reformatted utils.py.

143
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,199
Flake8 then passes.

144
00:08:07,519 --> 00:08:08,671
Pause and run it.

145
00:08:08,916 --> 00:08:11,772
git diff shows exactly what Black changed:

146
00:08:11,772 --> 00:08:15,103
a space after the comma,
and one blank line gone.

147
00:08:15,350 --> 00:08:17,590
Run it again, and every hook passes.

148
00:08:17,910 --> 00:08:19,531
Nothing is left to fix.

149
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:23,377
Now git add .,
and git commit with a message:

150
00:08:23,377 --> 00:08:25,433
Format utils.py with Black.

151
00:08:25,753 --> 00:08:28,398
The message is ours; the README has none.

152
00:08:28,718 --> 00:08:30,638
The hooks run on the commit and pass.

153
00:08:30,958 --> 00:08:32,110
Then git push.

154
00:08:32,430 --> 00:08:33,859
Pause and push yours.

155
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:35,913
Run 2: all six green.

156
00:08:36,233 --> 00:08:40,841
In the lint job, Black is happy,
and this time Flake8 runs too, and passes.

157
00:08:41,161 --> 00:08:44,084
On a fork, this is your first run,
and it's green.

158
00:08:44,333 --> 00:08:47,042
Now your run matches
the README's screenshot.

159
00:08:47,362 --> 00:08:48,773
One turn of the loop:

160
00:08:48,773 --> 00:08:52,333
a red notification,
a fix, a commit, and a green run.

161
00:08:53,783 --> 00:08:56,535
The lecture calls this one
the local conundrum.

162
00:08:56,855 --> 00:09:01,612
If the hooks already ran on your laptop,
why run the same checks again on a server?

163
00:09:01,850 --> 00:09:04,645
These steps are extras,
and you can just watch.

164
00:09:04,965 --> 00:09:09,203
A quick edit squeezes
the spaces out of one line in service.py,

165
00:09:09,203 --> 00:09:10,682
and git diff shows it.

166
00:09:10,916 --> 00:09:14,116
git add ., then git commit -m "quick fix".

167
00:09:14,436 --> 00:09:18,746
The Black hook fails and reformats the file,
so the hook stopped the commit.

168
00:09:19,683 --> 00:09:23,566
git status --short shows the letter M,
for modified, twice.

169
00:09:23,886 --> 00:09:28,238
Black fixed your working copy,
but the staged copy is still the messy one.

170
00:09:28,483 --> 00:09:33,219
Now the lecture's command:
git commit --no-verify -m "quick fix".

171
00:09:33,539 --> 00:09:38,574
No hook ran, and the commit exists:
--no-verify skips every pre-commit check.

172
00:09:38,850 --> 00:09:39,850
We push it.

173
00:09:40,109 --> 00:09:43,586
Run 3: lint and format, red, on service.py.

174
00:09:43,906 --> 00:09:46,018
The tests and the summary all pass.

175
00:09:46,683 --> 00:09:49,904
The skipped Flake8 would have flagged
that line three times.

176
00:09:50,224 --> 00:09:55,110
CI caught what --no-verify let through:
slide 31, at your terminal.

177
00:09:55,350 --> 00:09:56,843
Clean up the experiment.

178
00:09:57,163 --> 00:10:01,451
Here, git restore only throws away
Black's fix in the working copy.

179
00:10:01,771 --> 00:10:03,110
If you followed along,

180
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:06,273
the messy commit is on your main now,
pushed or not.

181
00:10:06,593 --> 00:10:07,787
Not pushed yet?

182
00:10:08,107 --> 00:10:11,009
git reset --hard HEAD~1 drops it.

183
00:10:11,329 --> 00:10:12,329
Pushed?

184
00:10:12,395 --> 00:10:17,664
Run pre-commit run --all-files,
then add, commit and push, before part 6.

185
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:22,892
But CI repeats only Black and Flake8,
and only on the api/ folder.

186
00:10:23,212 --> 00:10:28,876
The seven hygiene hooks never run in CI:
trailing whitespace, end of file,

187
00:10:28,876 --> 00:10:33,238
YAML, large files,
JSON, merge markers, and private keys.

188
00:10:33,483 --> 00:10:37,408
One more gap: the hooks pin an older
Black than the image installs.

189
00:10:37,728 --> 00:10:41,483
At the time of recording they agreed,
but they can drift apart.

190
00:10:41,733 --> 00:10:46,320
A teammate commits a private key file
with --no-verify and pushes to main.

191
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:48,858
Which job of this pipeline catches it?

192
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:01,540
None. The detect-private-key hook
is a local hook only.

193
00:11:01,860 --> 00:11:05,490
The fix is one CI step,
pre-commit run --all-files:

194
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:10,116
the very step that fails
on the lecture's imagined Slack message.

195
00:11:10,436 --> 00:11:13,188
And rotate that key; it's already on GitHub.

196
00:11:14,533 --> 00:11:18,266
That imagined message brings us back
to the lecture's webhooks.

197
00:11:18,586 --> 00:11:21,934
A webhook is GitHub
calling an outside address

198
00:11:21,934 --> 00:11:23,608
when something happens:

199
00:11:23,608 --> 00:11:26,949
to start an outside CI
system such as Jenkins,

200
00:11:26,949 --> 00:11:29,274
to deploy, or to post to a chat.

201
00:11:29,516 --> 00:11:33,057
This part is optional:
it needs your own Slack workspace.

202
00:11:33,377 --> 00:11:35,809
And this webhook works the other way round.

203
00:11:36,129 --> 00:11:39,067
Slack gives you an incoming webhook,
an address,

204
00:11:39,067 --> 00:11:42,188
and the last steps
of the test summary job call it.

205
00:11:42,508 --> 00:11:45,388
GitHub doesn't ring this bell;
your workflow does.

206
00:11:45,633 --> 00:11:49,546
The lecture imagines a Slack
channel told about every push,

207
00:11:49,546 --> 00:11:53,590
failures included, with the failed step,
counts and duration.

208
00:11:53,910 --> 00:11:57,942
This workflow sends less:
passed or failed, the repository,

209
00:11:57,942 --> 00:12:02,316
branch, commit and author,
one mark per test job, and two links.

210
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:04,534
The webhook address is a secret.

211
00:12:04,854 --> 00:12:08,918
Add it under Settings →
Secrets and variables → Actions,

212
00:12:08,918 --> 00:12:14,070
as a new repository secret named
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL, not under Webhooks.

213
00:12:14,390 --> 00:12:18,187
It never goes in the repository,
logs show it as ***,

214
00:12:18,187 --> 00:12:21,195
and pull requests from forks don't get it.

215
00:12:21,433 --> 00:12:24,145
The check step tests
whether the secret is set,

216
00:12:24,145 --> 00:12:26,510
and writes enabled=true or enabled=false.

217
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:29,305
Why not test the secret
right in the if line?

218
00:12:29,625 --> 00:12:31,812
GitHub doesn't allow secrets there;

219
00:12:31,812 --> 00:12:36,622
the repository's own history tried it,
and replaced it with this shell check.

220
00:12:36,866 --> 00:12:39,949
In run 1 there's no secret,
so enabled is false,

221
00:12:39,949 --> 00:12:42,968
and the Slack step is skipped,
as it should be.

222
00:12:43,216 --> 00:12:44,496
Now the demo run.

223
00:12:44,816 --> 00:12:46,523
Check test results fails.

224
00:12:46,843 --> 00:12:50,284
The check step has no if:
always(), so it's skipped too,

225
00:12:50,284 --> 00:12:54,032
enabled stays empty,
and the Slack step's condition is false.

226
00:12:54,266 --> 00:12:56,997
As written,
the bell only rings for good news.

227
00:12:57,317 --> 00:13:00,495
The fix is one line: if:
always(), on the check step.

228
00:13:00,815 --> 00:13:03,354
The exercises have a hardened version.

229
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:08,476
Part 6 of the README is optional,
and it's the lecture's gate.

230
00:13:08,796 --> 00:13:11,527
A push-triggered run
is the post-receive bell.

231
00:13:11,847 --> 00:13:16,048
On GitHub.com,
the gate that refuses is branch protection,

232
00:13:16,048 --> 00:13:18,076
with required status checks.

233
00:13:18,317 --> 00:13:21,432
Go to Settings → Branches,
and add a rule for main.

234
00:13:21,752 --> 00:13:24,674
Tick the boxes "Require
a pull request before merging",

235
00:13:24,674 --> 00:13:27,384
and "Require status checks
to pass before merging".

236
00:13:27,704 --> 00:13:32,034
Today's page may call it a classic rule,
or offer rulesets instead.

237
00:13:32,283 --> 00:13:33,584
Then pick the checks.

238
00:13:33,904 --> 00:13:37,490
A small slip:
the picker lists jobs by their names,

239
00:13:37,490 --> 00:13:41,990
like Lint and Format Check and Unit Tests,
not the README's IDs.

240
00:13:42,310 --> 00:13:44,507
They appear only after a recent run.

241
00:13:44,827 --> 00:13:48,027
On a private repository
this needs a paid plan.

242
00:13:48,267 --> 00:13:52,342
Then test it:
git checkout -b feature/test-protection.

243
00:13:52,662 --> 00:13:56,447
Pushing that branch starts nothing,
as you saw with the triggers;

244
00:13:56,447 --> 00:13:59,125
the run starts
when you open the pull request.

245
00:13:59,367 --> 00:14:03,356
The README breaks the tests
by appending def broken(): assert False.

246
00:14:03,676 --> 00:14:06,877
An extra run in the image
still says 11 passed,

247
00:14:06,877 --> 00:14:11,441
because pytest only collects functions
whose names start with test.

248
00:14:11,683 --> 00:14:14,819
So name it test_broken instead,
an extra fix.

249
00:14:15,139 --> 00:14:17,166
Now 1 failed and 11 passed.

250
00:14:17,486 --> 00:14:20,216
That's the one
that turns the pull request red,

251
00:14:20,216 --> 00:14:23,353
and with protection on,
the merge button stays locked.

252
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:27,724
Then git add .,
git commit -m "Intentionally break tests",

253
00:14:27,724 --> 00:14:30,853
and git push origin feature/test-protection.

254
00:14:31,173 --> 00:14:36,123
Open the pull request on GitHub:
the merge stays locked until the tests pass.

255
00:14:36,443 --> 00:14:38,128
Pause and try it on yours.

256
00:14:38,448 --> 00:14:41,093
Here, git checkout --
puts the test file back.

257
00:14:42,233 --> 00:14:44,252
The twist the lecture promised:

258
00:14:44,252 --> 00:14:48,420
non-determinism enters every stage
when the code trains a model.

259
00:14:48,740 --> 00:14:53,233
Its answers: version everything;
automate retraining and testing,

260
00:14:53,233 --> 00:14:57,380
which Milestone 5 asks for;
monitor; and roll out with care.

261
00:14:57,616 --> 00:15:01,051
Continuous deployment,
the lecture's last section,

262
00:15:01,051 --> 00:15:03,867
automates the release: that's lecture 23.

263
00:15:04,116 --> 00:15:05,652
Clean up: switch to main,

264
00:15:05,652 --> 00:15:08,724
and delete
the feature/test-protection branch here

265
00:15:08,724 --> 00:15:11,391
and on GitHub; that closes its pull request.

266
00:15:11,711 --> 00:15:13,017
Remove the branch rule,

267
00:15:13,017 --> 00:15:15,913
and the Slack secret and webhook,
if you made them.

268
00:15:16,233 --> 00:15:19,081
Disable the workflow
on a private repository,

269
00:15:19,081 --> 00:15:21,738
and run the Docker cleanup from lecture 9.

270
00:15:21,983 --> 00:15:23,242
Once more, the loop.

271
00:15:23,562 --> 00:15:25,034
Your push is the event.

272
00:15:25,354 --> 00:15:29,706
Each job gets a fresh machine,
so the image travels as an artifact.

273
00:15:30,026 --> 00:15:32,970
Every step is a gate
that reads an exit code,

274
00:15:32,970 --> 00:15:35,914
and the summary hears
only the jobs it needs.

275
00:15:36,150 --> 00:15:38,091
Test yourself, pausing on each.

276
00:15:38,411 --> 00:15:41,896
One: name two ways code
reaches the shared repository

277
00:15:41,896 --> 00:15:44,065
without passing your local hooks.

278
00:15:44,385 --> 00:15:46,539
Which checks does this pipeline repeat?

279
00:15:46,783 --> 00:15:50,388
Two: which unit gets a fresh machine,
a job or a step?

280
00:15:50,708 --> 00:15:53,972
And why does that force a docker load
in every check job?

281
00:15:54,216 --> 00:15:58,184
Three: your run is red,
but the summary says all tests passed.

282
00:15:58,504 --> 00:16:00,766
Why, and what is the one-line fix?

283
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,125
Four:
why can Flake8 fail without failing its job,

284
00:16:04,125 --> 00:16:07,250
and why don't you see its output
when Black fails?

285
00:16:07,983 --> 00:16:12,055
Five: is a push-triggered run a pre-receive
or a post-receive check,

286
00:16:12,055 --> 00:16:13,551
and what makes it a gate?

287
00:16:13,871 --> 00:16:17,050
Answers are in the exercises
and the study guide.

